      "body": "By Urmimala Roy, Xi-Wei Lin, and Ravi Todi\n\nTwo years ago, we introduced why the industry was moving to gate‑all‑around (GAA) transistors and why Foundation IP co-optimized with electronic design automation (EDA) flows would be key to success to making that transition work. Since then, GAA has moved from early adoption into the mainstream of leading-edge design—and the industry’s attention is already turning to what comes next.\n\nAs designs push past the 2nm node, the architectures that carried CMOS scaling for decades are reaching their practical limits, and the demands of AI computing are adding to the pressure. Each new node must deliver better power, performance, and area (PPA) while managing new physical realities, from quantized device widths and weakening electrostatic control to the thermal challenges of backside power delivery. Increasingly, meeting those targets depends not on the transistor alone, but on how closely Foundation IP and EDA flows are co-optimized for the target ",
